// The bot path must not change behaviour (plan review item 8).
//
// tests/fixtures/bot-golden/contain-plans.json and egress-transcripts.json are
// snapshots of what scripts/pi-bots/pi_contain.mjs and pi_egress.mjs produce.
// They were RECORDED ONCE, at the base sha, and are never regenerated by a
// later PR: a green run here means a refactor changed nothing observable. Any
// diff to either golden file (or to this test's normalisation) must be
// rejected in review — a regenerated golden proves nothing.
//
// ONE recorded exception: contain-plans.json was re-recorded for the contain
// tier's masked-folder fix (2026-11-19 inventory, B4). The whole diff is, per
// masked directory, an appended `--remount-ro <dir>` (a mask is now an empty
// READ-ONLY mount, so a write into another project's folder fails instead of
// vanishing into scratch), plus one redundant `--ro-bind` pair dropped in the
// "MCP servers" case because the command's install root is already inside the
// server's bound cwd. Nothing else moved; tests/pi-contain-plan.test.js pins the
// new mask shape directly, so the property is not only golden-derived.
//
// A SECOND recorded exception (security review, 2026-11-20): contain-plans.json
// was re-recorded again for the data-dir CREATION fix. Every reserved data-dir
// name and every installed bundle id is now masked whether or not it exists at
// spawn (a bot must not be able to create `<data>/<installed id>/.venv`, code a
// host-side uv then runs), so each case that binds a data dir gained one
// `--tmpfs <data>/<name>` plus its trailing `--remount-ro <data>/<name>` per
// absent reserved name.
//
// What the re-record was checked against, so a reviewer knows what to look for
// in the diff: the same cases in the same order, every `refused` list and `env`
// object identical, and every token of the old argv still present, in order.
// The ONLY thing a correct diff may therefore contain is added `--tmpfs` /
// `--remount-ro` pairs on data-dir paths — any other change (a moved token, a
// different refusal line, a dropped bind) is a bug, not the fix.
// tests/pi-contain-plan.test.js pins the property directly (B11), so it is not
// only golden-derived.
//
// A THIRD recorded exception (contain-tier review, 2026-11-21): re-recorded for
// the mount-point fix. The plan no longer creates the mount point of an
// absent-entry mask as a side effect (a swallowed `mkdirSync` — nothing checked
// that the directory existed when bwrap ran, and a spawn without it ran with a
// trusted data-dir name reachable, which is what
// `<data>/<installed id>/.venv` succeeding in a real sandbox looked like). The
// plan now NAMES those mount points and the spawn path makes them
// (`ensureContainMountPoints`, refusing the spawn on a symlink, a file, another
// uid or a failure), and `assembleBindArgs` emits the `--remount-ro` ops in PATH
// order. What the re-record changed is therefore EXACTLY this: the order of the
// `--remount-ro` ops (they used to follow the order the masks happened to be
// recorded in — readdir order for data-dir entries that existed at spawn, so a
// host's filesystem decided part of the argv). Mask sets, tokens, `env`,
// `refused` and case order are unchanged; the re-record was diffed case by case
// against the previous file and the only delta was that ordering.
//
// A FOURTH recorded exception (item: bots never read Crow's identity file):
// re-recorded because the sharing identity is now masked UNCONDITIONALLY. The
// contain plan used to keep `<dataDir>/identity.json` when a bot's definition
// named `crow-sharing` tools (`unmaskIdentity`), and that opt-out is gone — the
// file holds the master seed every instance and bot key derives from. The whole
// diff is therefore ONE case: the second of the two cases recorded for the
// "instance data dir read-write with its secrets masked" title (the one built
// with the retired option) is now byte-identical to the first, i.e. it carries
// the `--ro-bind /dev/null <data>/identity.json` mask it used to lack. Same case
// count and order, same names, every other plan untouched. The property itself
// is pinned directly by tests/pi-contain-plan.test.js, which also asserts the
// retired option and a keep-list naming the file change nothing, so it is not
// only golden-derived.
//
// A FOURTH recorded exception (security review, H5): re-recorded once for the
// `runtimes` reservation. The native runtime's install root
// (`<data>/runtimes/llamacpp/<release>/…`) is now a RESERVED data-dir name, so
// every case that binds a data dir gained exactly one `--tmpfs` + one
// `--remount-ro` pair on `<data>/runtimes` — nothing else moved: same cases in
// the same order, every other token identical, and the property itself is pinned
// directly by tests/pi-contain-plan.test.js (which asserts `runtimes` is reserved,
// masked and not a FILE-shaped name), so it is not only golden-derived.
//
// A FIFTH recorded exception (H1, structural masks): `<data>/projects` is masked wholesale (one
// `--tmpfs` + one `--remount-ro`) and the bot's own workspace is bound back inside it, replacing
// the per-project masks (`<data>/projects/<other>`) the fixtures carried. Only contain-plans.json
// moved; egress-transcripts.json is byte-identical. Pinned directly by
// tests/pi-contain-plan.test.js (C9, B4) and tests/contain-structural-masks.test.js (real bwrap).
//
// The count, so a reviewer knows the `--tmpfs` volume is the intended one and
// not a leak: 33 masks per case that binds a data dir (35 reserved names minus
// the two FILE-shaped ones — `identity.json` and `instance-id` are never
// created), 32 in the one case whose fixture keeps one (the C9 keep-list); `projects`
// is one of the 33 and is masked whole with the bot's own workspace bound back inside it. NOT ONE
// installed-bundle-id mask, because none of these fixture instances records an
// absent installed id. 1121 data-dir masks in total (33 cases of 33, one of 32); nothing in the plan reads
// the repository's `registry/add-ons.json`. tests/pi-contain-plan.test.js (B11c)
// asserts that source rule directly.
import { test } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { dirname, join } from "node:path";
import { fileURLToPath } from "node:url";

import { buildContainCases, buildEgressTranscripts } from "./fixtures/bot-golden/cases.mjs";

const GOLDEN_DIR = join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), "fixtures", "bot-golden");
const MISSING = "golden not recorded: run node tests/fixtures/bot-golden/record.mjs";

function readGolden(name) {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(readFileSync(join(GOLDEN_DIR, name), "utf8"));
  } catch (e) {
    if (e && e.code === "ENOENT") assert.fail(MISSING);
    throw e;
  }
}

test("the bot contain plan is byte-identical to the recorded golden", () => {
  const golden = readGolden("contain-plans.json");
  assert.deepEqual(buildContainCases(), golden);
});

test("the bot egress relay's transcripts are byte-identical to the recorded golden", async () => {
  const golden = readGolden("egress-transcripts.json");
  assert.deepEqual(await buildEgressTranscripts(), golden);
});